# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Weathra

# The template above is the Python .gitignore. Two of its unanchored patterns — `lib/` and
# `build/` — would otherwise swallow real frontend source, so anchor them to the backend and
# re-include what the frontend owns.
!frontend/lib/
!frontend/lib/**

# Frontend build output and tooling artifacts.
frontend/.next/
frontend/out/
frontend/coverage/
frontend/playwright-report/
frontend/test-results/
frontend/next-env.d.ts
frontend/node_modules/

# ------------------------------------------------------- Weathra: secret safety
#
# The repository is public, so this section is load-bearing rather than tidy.
# Private environment files are ignored; the committed `.env.example` templates
# are not, and the negations below keep them that way.

# Private environment files, at the repository root and in either application.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.development
.env.development.local
.env.production
.env.production.local
.env.test.local
backend/.env
weathra/backend/.env
frontend/.env.local
weathra/frontend/.env.local

# The committed templates. Re-included explicitly so a broader pattern added
# above — now or later — cannot quietly stop them being committable.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example
!.env.template
!**/.env.template

# Credential and key material. None of this belongs in the tree at all; the
# patterns exist so an accidental download or export cannot be staged.
*.pem
*.key
*.keystore
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
id_rsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519
.netrc
.pgpass
secrets.json
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
credentials.json
client_secret*.json
service-account*.json
*-service-account.json
gcp-*.json
*.sa.json
.supabase/
.gcloud/
